00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:07.100
So, you've learned about gradient descent, about multiple linear regression, and also

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.100
vectorization.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:13.040
Let's put it all together to implement gradient descent for multiple linear regression with

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.340
vectorization.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.340
This would be cool.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.440
Let's quickly review what multiple linear regression looks like.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.940
Using our previous notation, let's see how you can write it more succinctly using vector

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.080
notation.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.320
We have parameters w1 to wn, as well as b.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:36.320
But instead of thinking of w1 to wn as separate numbers, that is, separate parameters, let's

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:44.920
instead collect all of the w's into a vector w, so that now w is a vector of length n.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:51.080
So we're just going to think of the parameters of this model as a vector w, as well as b,

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.480
where b is still a number, same as before.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.000
For us before, we had defined multiple linear regression like this.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:07.360
Now using vector notation, we can write the model as f sub wb of x equals the vector w

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.080
dot product with the vector x plus b.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.320
And remember that this dot here means dot product.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.320
Our cost function can be defined as j of w1 through wn, comma, b.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:28.240
But instead of just thinking of j as a function of these n different parameters wj, as well

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:36.520
as b, we're going to write j as a function of parameter vector w and the number b.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:45.800
So this w1 through wn is replaced by this vector w, and j now takes its input, a vector

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.400
w and a number b, and returns a number.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.600
So this is what gradient descent looks like.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:59.560
We're going to repeatedly update each parameter wj to be wj minus alpha times the derivative

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:08.200
of the cost j, where j has parameters w1 through wn and b, and once again we just write this

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.840
as j of vector w and number b.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.320
Let's see what this will look like when you implement gradient descent, and in particular,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.600
let's take a look at the derivative term.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.520
We'll see that gradient descent becomes just a little bit different with multiple features

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.320
compared to just one feature.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.640
Here's what we had when we had gradient descent with one feature.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:40.080
We had an update rule for w and a separate update rule for b, so hopefully these look

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.160
familiar to you.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:48.240
And this term here is the derivative of the cost function j with respect to the parameter

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:55.600
w, and similarly we have an update rule for parameter b with univariate regression, we

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.120
had only one feature.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.720
We call that feature xi without any subscript.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:09.200
Now here's the new notation for when we have n features where n is two or more.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.760
We get this update rule for gradient descent.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:21.520
We get w1 to be w1 minus alpha times this expression here, and this formula is actually

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.320
the derivative of the cost j with respect to w1.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:33.080
The formula for the derivative of j with respect to w1 on the right looks very similar to the

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.320
case of one feature on the left.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.960
The error term still takes a prediction, f of x minus the target y.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:49.480
One difference is that w and x are now vectors, and just as w on the left has now become

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:57.920
w1 here on the right, xi here on the left is now instead xi subscript 1 here on the

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:05.040
right, and this is just for j equals 1.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:12.480
For multiple linear regression, we have j ranging from 1 through n, and so we'll update

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:24.240
the parameters w1, w2 all the way up to wn, and then as before we'll update b.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.640
And if you implement this, you get gradient descent for multiple regression.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.600
So that's it for gradient descent for multiple regression.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:41.200
Before moving on from this video, I want to make a quick aside or a quick side note on

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:48.560
an alternative way for finding w and b for linear regression, and this method is called

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.320
the normal equation.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.160
Whereas it turns out gradient descent is a great method for minimizing the cost function

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.600
j to find w and b, there is one other algorithm that works only for linear regression, and

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.560
pretty much none of the other algorithms you see in this specialization for solving for

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:09.840
w and b.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.960
And this other method does not need an iterative gradient descent algorithm.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.800
Called the normal equation method, it turns out to be possible to use an advanced linear

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.260
algebra library to just solve for w and b all in one go without iterations.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:32.620
Some disadvantages of the normal equation method are, first, unlike gradient descent,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:37.220
this does not generalize to other learning algorithms, such as the logistic regression

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.940
algorithm that you learn about next week, or the neural networks or other algorithms

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.520
you see later in this specialization.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.980
The normal equation method is also quite slow if the number of features n is large.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:56.780
Almost no machine learning practitioners should implement the normal equation method themselves.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:03.380
But if you are using a mature machine learning library and call linear regression, there is

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:08.580
a chance that on the back end, it will be using this to solve for w and b.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.980
So if you are ever in a job interview and hear the term normal equation, that's what

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.320
this refers to.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.620
Don't worry about the details of how the normal equation works.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:24.540
Just be aware that some machine learning libraries may use this complicated method in the back

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.700
end to solve for w and b.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.260
But for most learning algorithms, including how you implement linear regression yourself,

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:36.300
gradient descent is often a better way to get the job done.

